//! Zamani Quantum Routing — Movement Primitives
//!
//! Production routing movement namespace.
//!
//! # Responsibility
//!
//! This module defines and exports the movement layer used by the Zamani
//! quantum routing subsystem.
//!
//! Movement is deliberately separated from:
//!
//! - topology construction;
//! - logical/physical mapping ownership;
//! - routing algorithms;
//! - layout selection;
//! - cost calculation;
//! - hardware provider APIs;
//! - gate decomposition;
//! - scheduling;
//! - pulse generation;
//! - execution;
//! - simulation;
//! - QEC decoding.
//!
//! The movement layer answers one specific question:
//!
//! > "What physical movement/permutation operation does a routing algorithm
//! > want to apply to the current logical-to-physical placement?"
//!
//! # Architecture
//!
//! ```text
//!                         Quantum IR
//!                             │
//!                             ▼
//!                         Layout
//!                             │
//!                             ▼
//!                    Routing Algorithm
//!                             │
//!                             ▼
//!                     Routing Candidate
//!                             │
//!                             ▼
//!                    ┌─────────────────┐
//!                    │  routing::moves │
//!                    └────────┬────────┘
//!                             │
//!              ┌──────────────┼──────────────┐
//!              ▼              ▼              ▼
//!            Swap          Bridge       Permutation
//!              │              │              │
//!              └──────────────┼──────────────┘
//!                             ▼
//!                     Mapping Transition
//!                             │
//!                             ▼
//!                    Routing Verification
//!                             │
//!                             ▼
//!                    Hardware Lowering
//! ```
//!
//! # Important semantic boundary
//!
//! A movement operation is NOT necessarily a hardware instruction.
//!
//! For example:
//!
//! ```text
//! RoutingMove::Swap(p0, p1)
//! ```
//!
//! means:
//!
//!     "exchange the logical states occupying physical locations p0 and p1"
//!
//! It does not mean that the target device necessarily has a native `SWAP`
//! instruction.
//!
//! A later hardware-lowering stage may implement that movement as:
//!
//! ```text
//! SWAP
//!     │
//!     ├── native SWAP
//!     ├── 3 × CX
//!     ├── provider-specific decomposition
//!     └── another supported equivalent
//! ```
//!
//! This separation is essential for keeping routing backend-independent.
//!
//! # Movement categories
//!
//! The production movement namespace contains three primary primitives:
//!
//! - [`swap`] — exchange two adjacent physical locations;
//! - [`bridge`] — represent a mediated/bridge-style interaction when the
//!   target capability explicitly permits it;
//! - [`permutation`] — represent a composed logical-state permutation.
//!
//! # Stable public API
//!
//! Algorithms should normally depend on the re-exported types from this
//! module rather than reaching into implementation files:
//!
//! ```text
//! crate::quantum::routing::moves::SwapMove
//! crate::quantum::routing::moves::BridgeMove
//! crate::quantum::routing::moves::QubitPermutation
//! ```
//!
//! This allows the implementation of an individual movement primitive to
//! evolve without requiring routing algorithms to change their imports.
//!
//! # Integration with foundational routing types
//!
//! The foundational routing vocabulary lives in:
//!
//! ```text
//! crate::quantum::routing::types
//! ```
//!
//! In particular, movement implementations must use:
//!
//! - `PhysicalQubitId`;
//! - `LogicalQubitId`;
//! - `RoutingOperation`;
//! - `QubitPermutation`-compatible semantics;
//! - gate identity and routing-level identifiers.
//!
//! Movement implementations must NOT introduce another physical-qubit ID
//! representation.
//!
//! # Integration with topology
//!
//! Movement legality is ultimately determined by:
//!
//! ```text
//! crate::quantum::routing::topology
//! ```
//!
//! A movement implementation must never assume that every pair of physical
//! qubits is connected.
//!
//! In particular, a `SwapMove` must be validated against the target topology
//! before being committed.
//!
//! # Integration with mapping
//!
//! Mapping state is owned by:
//!
//! ```text
//! crate::quantum::routing::mapping
//! ```
//!
//! Movement primitives must not maintain a second authoritative mapping.
//!
//! Their responsibility is to describe a transition.
//!
//! The mapping subsystem is responsible for applying that transition to the
//! authoritative logical-to-physical and physical-to-logical state.
//!
//! This prevents the following class of bugs:
//!
//! ```text
//! routing algorithm mapping
//!          ≠
//! hardware mapping
//!          ≠
//! result mapping
//! ```
//!
//! There must be one authoritative mapping.
//!
//! # Integration with routing algorithms
//!
//! Algorithms such as:
//!
//! - basic routing;
//! - shortest-path routing;
//! - lookahead;
//! - SABRE;
//! - LightSABRE-style heuristics;
//! - noise-aware routing;
//! - dynamic routing;
//! - future custom/plugin algorithms
//!
//! produce movement candidates.
//!
//! They should use this namespace for movement validation and representation.
//!
//! The movement layer must remain algorithm-neutral.
//!
//! # Integration with cost models
//!
//! Movement primitives expose enough information for:
//!
//! ```text
//! crate::quantum::routing::cost
//! ```
//!
//! to calculate:
//!
//! - movement count;
//! - depth contribution;
//! - duration;
//! - estimated error;
//! - fidelity impact;
//! - weighted objective contribution.
//!
//! The movement layer must NOT implement a routing objective itself.
//!
//! # Integration with verification
//!
//! The verification subsystem must be able to inspect every movement through
//! the stable movement interfaces exported here.
//!
//! Verification must be able to establish:
//!
//! 1. every referenced physical qubit exists;
//! 2. every required physical connection exists;
//! 3. every movement is legal for the selected routing target;
//! 4. every permutation is bijective;
//! 5. mapping transitions are consistent;
//! 6. movement sequences do not contain impossible state transitions.
//!
//! # Transactionality
//!
//! A movement object should be treated as an immutable description of a
//! proposed state transition.
//!
//! The preferred routing flow is:
//!
//! ```text
//! candidate movement
//!        │
//!        ▼
//! validate
//!        │
//!        ▼
//! speculative mapping transition
//!        │
//!        ▼
//! verify
//!        │
//!    ┌───┴───┐
//!    │       │
//! commit   rollback
//! ```
//!
//! Movement primitives must not perform hidden global mutations.
//!
//! # Directed hardware
//!
//! Physical adjacency and gate direction are separate concepts.
//!
//! A physical edge may be usable for a SWAP while a directional two-qubit
//! gate is legal in only one direction.
//!
//! Therefore `SwapMove` must represent physical state movement and must not
//! inherit the directional semantics of the gate being routed.
//!
//! Direction-sensitive gate legality belongs to the topology/hardware layer.
//!
//! # Multi-qubit operations
//!
//! Movement primitives must not silently decompose arbitrary multi-qubit
//! gates.
//!
//! For example:
//!
//! ```text
//! CCX
//! ```
//!
//! is a gate-synthesis/decomposition concern unless the target hardware
//! explicitly exposes a native operation.
//!
//! Routing may move the participating logical qubits, but gate decomposition
//! belongs to the appropriate synthesis/lowering stage.
//!
//! # Distributed quantum computing
//!
//! The movement API deliberately uses the abstract concept of a physical
//! location rather than assuming that all physical qubits belong to one chip.
//!
//! Future distributed routing can therefore introduce movement semantics such
//! as:
//!
//! - inter-module movement;
//! - teleportation;
//! - entanglement-assisted movement;
//! - network-mediated routing;
//! - remote-state permutation.
//!
//! Those mechanisms must be introduced as explicit movement types rather than
//! changing the meaning of `SwapMove`.
//!
//! # No unsafe
//!
//! This module contains no `unsafe` code and must remain safe Rust.
//!
//! # Rust compatibility
//!
//! Target:
//!
//! - Rust 1.97;
//! - Rust 1.97.1;
//! - Rust 2021 edition.
//!
//! No nightly features are required.
//!
//! # Dependency policy
//!
//! This module should contain only module declarations, stable re-exports,
//! documentation, and namespace-level compile-time tests.
//!
//! It must not depend on:
//!
//! - provider SDKs;
//! - network I/O;
//! - operating-system APIs;
//! - compiler parser internals;
//! - OpenQASM;
//! - simulation engines;
//! - benchmarking implementations;
//! - random-number generators.
//!
//! Concrete dependencies belong to the corresponding movement implementation.
//!
//! # File-completion contract
//!
//! Once this file is established, the following files must conform to its
//! public namespace:
//!
//! ```text
//! moves/swap.rs
//! moves/bridge.rs
//! moves/permutation.rs
//! ```
//!
//! Later implementation work must not require changing this namespace merely
//! because an algorithm is added.
//!
//! The intended dependency direction is:
//!
//! ```text
//! types.rs
//!    │
//!    ├──────────────┐
//!    ▼              ▼
//! topology.rs    mapping.rs
//!    │              │
//!    └──────┬───────┘
//!           ▼
//!      moves/mod.rs
//!       │    │    │
//!       ▼    ▼    ▼
//!     swap bridge permutation
//!       │    │    │
//!       └────┼────┘
//!            ▼
//!       routing algorithms
//!            │
//!            ▼
//!          router
//!            │
//!            ▼
//!       verification
//! ```
//!
//! `moves/mod.rs` is therefore a namespace boundary, not another routing
//! algorithm.

#![deny(unsafe_code)]
#![deny(unsafe_op_in_unsafe_fn)]
#![deny(unused_must_use)]

/// Physical SWAP movement primitive.
///
/// `SwapMove` represents exchange of the logical states occupying two
/// physical locations. It is deliberately independent of whether the target
/// backend has a native SWAP gate.
pub mod swap;

/// Mediated/bridge-style movement or interaction primitive.
///
/// Bridge operations are capability-dependent. The implementation must never
/// assume that a bridge is universally supported by quantum hardware.
pub mod bridge;

/// Logical-state permutation produced by one or more movement operations.
///
/// This module provides the algebra needed to compose, invert, validate, and
/// apply movement-induced permutations.
pub mod permutation;

